  "comments": [
    {
      "position": 0,
      "source_id": null,
      "text": "耐える means to bear. \n\nVdic + に + 耐えない = (Verb) is unbearable",
      "html": "耐える means to bear. <br/><br/>Vdic + に + 耐えない = (Verb) is unbearable",
      "credits_raw": "dc",
      "content_sha256": "2c094be5a7ac622886dc1774e2e4ec40ce2adfdc56e693bccfea46c04ddf77d5",
      "original_created_at": null,
      "original_updated_at": null
    },
    {
      "position": 1,
      "source_id": null,
      "text": "On the difference between [nitaienai1] and [nitaienai2]\nThere are two たえないs of note in the Japanese language... hurray.　How to distinguish them?\n[nitaienai1] is for saying you just can't stand something and should be constructed from VERB辞書形 + にたえない\n[nitaienai2] is for saying that you have a strong emotional reaction to something - \"氏の当会へのご援助はまことに感謝に堪えません｜We cannot be too grateful to him for the help he has given our society.\" and should be constructed NOUN + たえない.\n\nBoth are on the old JLPT 1 syllabus, if anyone knows what's on the new N1, please advise",
      "html": "<b>On the difference between [nitaienai1] and [nitaienai2]</b><br/>There are two たえないs of note in the Japanese language... hurray.　How to distinguish them?<br/>[nitaienai1] is for saying you just can't stand something and should be constructed from VERB辞書形 + にたえない<br/>[nitaienai2] is for saying that you have a strong emotional reaction to something - \"氏の当会へのご援助はまことに感謝に堪えません｜We cannot be too grateful to him for the help he has given our society.\" and should be constructed NOUN + たえない.<br/><br/>Both are on the old JLPT 1 syllabus, if anyone knows what's on the new N1, please advise",
      "credits_raw": "LR",
      "content_sha256": "20eeef65c858bdd08a1273d5628361d9b196106cd83e40d5e22f324b002c1c0f",
      "original_created_at": null,
      "original_updated_at": null
    },
